---
import CaseStudyLayout from "@/layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import SectionHeading from "@/components/SectionHeading.astro";
import Figure from "@/components/Figure.astro";
import CTA from "@/components/CTA.astro";

const tocItems = [
  { id: "the-challenge", label: "The real challenge was trust" },
  { id: "guiding-principles", label: "Guiding principles" },
  { id: "onboarding", label: "Onboarding that builds confidence" },
  { id: "shopping", label: "Shopping across borders" },
  { id: "wallet", label: "The multi-currency wallet" },
  { id: "card", label: "The card experience" },
  { id: "crypto", label: "Crypto integration" },
  { id: "design-system", label: "The design system" },
  { id: "impact", label: "What the work demonstrated" },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Monetta: Building a Borderless Financial SuperApp"
  description="A product design case study: shaping the MVP of a borderless financial SuperApp, from token-based payments and multi-currency wallets to cross-border shopping and crypto."
  date="2020-07-08"
  dateFormatted="July 8, 2020"
  tocItems={tocItems}
>
  <p>
    Monetta is a borderless financial SuperApp I helped shape as the first product designer,
    working directly with the CEO. Access to global commerce and financial tools is still
    deeply unequal. Large parts of the world cannot reliably use international cards, pay or
    shop across borders, or reach global brands and modern financial products.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most existing solutions address only one narrow slice of the problem (a wallet here,
    remittances there, a prepaid card somewhere else) and break the moment someone tries to
    actually live with their money globally. Monetta's ambition was never to become another
    wallet. It was to become a financial ecosystem that lets people earn, store, spend, and
    move money freely across borders.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Role:</strong> First Product Designer (end-to-end product design, MVP
    strategy, information architecture, design systems, and close collaboration with the CEO
    on product direction).
  </p>

  <p>
    The question that guided everything was simple and unforgiving: "What is the smallest
    product that proves people will trust us with their money, and actually use it?"
  </p>

  <CTA links={[
    { label: "View live product", href: "https://www.monetta.es/" },
  ]} />

  <Figure
    src="/assets/images/Monetta01.png"
    alt="Monetta onboarding flow: splash screen with branding, phone number entry, and verification code input"
    caption="The onboarding flow: from splash screen to verification, designed to feel familiar and trustworthy."
    width={3528}
    height={1982}
    loading="eager"
  />

  <SectionHeading id="the-challenge" title="The real challenge was trust, not features" />

  <p>
    Monetta could have launched with a long list of capabilities: multi-currency wallet,
    token-based payments, global eCommerce access, digital service top-ups, debit cards,
    cross-border transfers. Each one was legitimate. Building all of them at once would have
    created a product that felt impressive in a pitch deck and fragile in people's hands.
  </p>

  <p>
    Financial products live or die on trust. People do not experiment casually with money
    the way they experiment with social apps. Every unclear label, every unexpected
    conversion, every moment of uncertainty compounds into hesitation. The work was less
    about adding surface area and more about reducing the distance between "this looks
    interesting" and "I am willing to put real value here."
  </p>

  <p>We treated the MVP as a proof of reliability rather than a showcase of technology.</p>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/images/Monetta06.png"
    alt="Monetta information architecture: a detailed flowchart mapping all flows"
    caption="The information architecture: mapping every flow across the SuperApp to keep complexity manageable."
    width={10584}
    height={5946}
  />

  <SectionHeading id="guiding-principles" title="Guiding principles that emerged" />

  <p>
    Three principles took shape through the early decisions and shaped every major product
    choice that followed.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Show value, not technology.</strong> Users should experience money that behaves
    predictably. The underlying mechanisms could be sophisticated; the interface had to feel
    ordinary and reliable.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Build confidence before ambition.</strong> Onboarding and early moments had to
    explain why Monetta exists, deliver tangible value quickly, and lower the emotional cost
    of trying something new with money.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Design a SuperApp that stays simple.</strong> The product needed a clear primary
    spine of actions, progressive disclosure of power, and structural patterns that could
    expand later without forcing users to relearn the product.
  </p>

  <SectionHeading id="onboarding" title="Onboarding that builds confidence, not hype" />

  <p>
    Traditional financial onboarding often oscillates between aggressive marketing language
    and dense compliance screens. We took a different route. Onboarding was treated as the
    first proof of the product's character. It explained why Monetta exists, showed concrete
    value early, and deliberately reduced fear around new financial systems.
  </p>

  <p>
    The goal was not excitement for its own sake. The goal was quiet confidence. This focus
    on early value and trust produced a measurable outcome: a <strong>25% increase in user
    retention</strong> through personalised product discovery. People stayed because the
    product demonstrated usefulness before it asked for deeper commitment.
  </p>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/images/Monetta09.png"
    alt="Monetta design system: primary and secondary color palettes, typography scale, icon set, UI elements"
    caption="The design system: colors, typography, icons, and component states that kept the product consistent as it grew."
    width={3528}
    height={1982}
  />

  <SectionHeading id="shopping" title="Shopping across borders" />

  <p>
    One of the earliest and most important choices was how to handle value itself.
    Token-based systems can easily become abstract and intimidating. We rejected that path.
    Tokens in Monetta were designed to behave like currency. Conversion was clear and
    reversible, and users saw value in terms they already understood rather than being asked
    to adopt a new mental model.
  </p>

  <p>
    No belief in the underlying technology was required, only reliability. This decision
    reduced cognitive load at the exact moment it mattered most: when someone first moved
    real money into the system. By making the representation of value feel familiar, we made
    the leap of trust smaller.
  </p>

  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta03.png" alt="Monetta store experience" caption="The store experience: browsing, searching, and discovering products with token-based pricing that feels like currency." width={3528} height={1982} />
  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta04.png" alt="Monetta product filters" caption="Product filtering: gender, price range, and category filters designed for clarity and speed." width={3528} height={1982} />
  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta05.png" alt="Monetta shopping flow" caption="From product detail to checkout: cart, order summary, and wishlist with consistent token-based pricing." width={3528} height={1982} />

  <SectionHeading id="wallet" title="The multi-currency wallet" />

  <p>
    At the center of the ecosystem sat the multi-currency wallet. Its job was not merely to
    hold balances. It was to help users preserve value. We designed and evolved the wallet
    experience so that people could keep value in USD and other stable references when local
    conditions made that desirable.
  </p>

  <p>
    The result was a <strong>40% increase in average wallet balances</strong>. People were
    not just transacting; they were trusting the product with meaningful amounts over time.
    That shift, from occasional use to storing value, is one of the strongest signals a
    financial product can receive.
  </p>

  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta07.png" alt="Monetta payment options" caption="Payment options: multiple local and international methods for adding funds, designed for the Latin American market." width={3528} height={1982} />
  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta08.png" alt="Monetta fiat wallet" caption="The fiat wallet: card balance, ATM withdrawals, and token-based value transfers in one clear flow." width={3528} height={1982} />

  <SectionHeading id="card" title="The card experience" />

  <p>
    A physical and virtual card brought Monetta into the real world. Users could select card
    designs, choose delivery methods, track shipment progress, and manage security controls,
    all within the same coherent experience. The card became the tangible proof that Monetta
    was real, not just another app promising future capability.
  </p>

  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta10.png" alt="Monetta card request flow" caption="Requesting a card: choose a design, pick a delivery method, and receive confirmation in three clear steps." width={3528} height={1982} />
  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta12.png" alt="Monetta card tracking" caption="Card tracking: clear delivery progress from order to doorstep." width={3528} height={1982} />
  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta11.png" alt="Monetta card management" caption="Card management: settings, profile, and security controls that put users in full control." width={3528} height={1982} />

  <SectionHeading id="crypto" title="Crypto integration" />

  <p>
    Leading end-to-end design across Wallet, Shopping, and Crypto integrations created
    consistent patterns that increased transaction frequency by <strong>30%</strong>. The
    crypto experience followed the same principle as everything else: show value in terms
    people understand, keep the interface predictable, and never force users to become
    experts before they can act.
  </p>

  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta13.png" alt="Monetta crypto portfolio" caption="Crypto portfolio: wallet integrations, live balances, and exchange in one unified experience." width={3528} height={1982} />
  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta14.png" alt="Monetta crypto trading" caption="Crypto trading: Bitcoin charts, token swaps, and USDT tracking with familiar financial patterns." width={3528} height={1982} />
  <Figure src="/assets/images/Monetta15.png" alt="Monetta crypto operations" caption="Crypto operations: QR scanning, point wallet overview, and request flows for receiving assets." width={3528} height={1982} />

  <SectionHeading id="design-system" title="A SuperApp without the weight of complexity" />

  <p>
    A SuperApp can easily become a dense collection of features competing for attention. We
    refused that outcome. The experience was organised around clear primary actions. Secondary
    capabilities appeared only when they became relevant. Progressive disclosure protected
    newcomers while still leaving room for power users.
  </p>

  <p>
    Structural patterns were established early so that future expansion (shopping, additional
    services, deeper integrations) would feel like natural extensions rather than bolted-on
    modules. This systems-level approach paid off. The product felt coherent even as its
    surface area grew.
  </p>

  <SectionHeading id="impact" title="What the work ultimately demonstrated" />

  <p>
    Monetta proved that a borderless financial product does not need to overwhelm users with
    every possible capability on day one. It needs to earn the right to expand by first
    becoming reliable, understandable, and useful in a smaller form.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>25%</strong> higher retention from an onboarding strategy centred on early value and trust</li>
    <li><strong>40%</strong> higher average wallet balances through a multi-currency experience focused on preserving value</li>
    <li><strong>30%</strong> higher transaction frequency from coherent system-level patterns across Wallet, Shopping, and Crypto</li>
    <li><strong>20%</strong> higher engagement from close partnership between design and leadership on product strategy</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    More important than the numbers was the qualitative shift. People began treating Monetta
    as a place where money could live, not just pass through. By prioritising clarity over
    spectacle, confidence over hype, and scalable simplicity over feature density, we created
    a foundation that could grow into a genuine borderless financial ecosystem without
    collapsing under its own complexity.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      In money products, trust is the real product. Everything else is infrastructure
      for earning it.
    </p>
    <cite>Masudur Rahman</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <Fragment slot="footer">
    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mt-0 mb-2">Key outcomes</h3>
    <ul class="mb-5">
      <li>25% higher retention, 40% higher wallet balances, 30% more transactions, 20% higher engagement</li>
      <li>End-to-end design across Wallet, Shopping, Crypto, and Card experiences as the first product designer</li>
      <li>Scalable design system and information architecture for a SuperApp that stays simple</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mb-5">
      If you're working on financial products, borderless commerce, or trust-first
      product design, I'd love to hear from you.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudyLayout>
